= Related Works <relatedworks>
Liu and Iftikhar combined dataflow partitioning, shared caching, pipelined execution, and multithreading in an @ETL optimization framework, achieving up to 4.7 times faster execution than conventional @ETL dataflows @LiuIftikhar2015. Liu et al. introduced ETLMR, adapting pygrametl to MapReduce for distributed dimensional @ETL. On an 80 GB dataset, increasing the number of parallel tasks from 4 to 20 reduced dimension processing time from 260.95 to 55.22 minutes and fact processing time from 230.44 to 50.66 minutes @Liu2011ETLMR. Kota and Rongala proposed a Python based @ETL optimization framework combining dynamic data partitioning with Dask, PySpark, and multiprocessing. Using TPC-H benchmarks, they reported up to 40% lower execution time and 30% improved resource utilization compared with static partitioning and sequential execution @KotaRongala2026. Dask also provides Dask Bag, a parallel collection designed for processing unstructured data such as text files and JSON records. It uses multiprocessing to distribute Python operations across multiple cores, avoiding the limitations imposed by the @GIL @DaskBag.

Radonić and Mekterović introduced ETLator, a Python-based ETL framework where pipelines are defined through scripts rather than a graphical interface. The framework supports common ETL transformations, multiple data sources, and parallel task execution, while also providing features such as caching and batch loading. Their evaluation demonstrated that a scripting-based approach could simplify ETL development while retaining the flexibility of Python @RadonicMekterovic2017. Dugré et al. compared Dask and Apache Spark using an 81 GB dataset and a real-world pipeline processing data from over 1,000 subjects. Their results showed comparable performance between the frameworks, with data transfer emerging as the main bottleneck and the @GIL limiting Dask in some workloads @Dugre2019. A subsequent study extended the evaluation to a 606 GB dataset, again identifying memory consumption and data transfer as important performance limitations @Dugre2023.

Raasveldt and Mühleisen introduced DuckDB, an embedded analytical database that supports parallel query execution through a vectorized execution engine. Its integration with Python allows SQL operations to be performed directly on in-memory data structures, avoiding the need for a separate database process or explicit data transfers @RaasveldtMuhleisen2019. Karagiannis et al. investigated scheduling strategies for ETL workflows, focusing on reducing execution time and memory consumption. They proposed combining data partitioning, pipelining, and parallel execution to improve workflow performance. Their evaluation showed that scheduling strategies tailored to different workflow structures could improve both execution time and memory utilization @Karagiannis2013.

Zholdybay and Aituov compared threading, multiprocessing, and hybrid execution across CPU-bound, I/O-bound, and mixed workloads. On four cores, multiprocessing achieved a 3.7 times speedup for CPU-bound tasks, but consumed approximately 350 MB compared with 110 MB for threading @ZholdybayAituov2025. For mixed workloads, a hybrid approach combining two processes with two threads achieved a 3.33 times speedup, approximately 14% faster than multiprocessing alone @ZholdybayAituov2025. Lion et al. investigated similar overheads in CPython and found that multiprocessing caused a 4.9 times slowdown in an indexed log-analysis benchmark due to serialization costs when transferring frequently accessed dictionaries between processes @Lion2022.

OMP4Py, introduced by Piñeiro and Pichel, provides OpenMP-style parallel programming through Python directives. Their evaluation demonstrated substantial differences between workloads. Numerical applications achieved a maximum speedup of 3.18 times with four threads, while the Wordcount workload reached 25.5 times speedup with 48 threads @OMP4Py2026. Montoya Salazar evaluated free-threaded CPython 3.14.2 against the conventional build, finding up to four times faster execution for parallel numerical workloads using six workers @MontoyaSalazar2026. However, shared mutable data could cause substantial slowdowns, reaching 12.18 times slower execution in one benchmark, highlighting the importance of synchronization and data sharing @MontoyaSalazar2026.

